---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const nav = [
  { label: 'Project Context', href: '#context' },
  { label: 'Opportunity', href: '#opportunity' },
  { label: 'Solution', href: '#relationships' },
  { label: 'Process', href: '#structure' },
  { label: 'Takeaway', href: '#takeaway' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Migration Dashboard"
  heading="Designing a self-service migration experience"
  role="Product Designer"
  team="1 Product Manager, 3 Developers"
  timeline="March 2026-Present"
  projectType="Product Design"
  nav={nav}
  commentId="case-study-migration"
>
  <section id="context" data-comment-id="migration-context">
    <p class="lead">
      Helping teams independently configure complex migration projects through a clearer and more
      scalable workflow.
    </p>

    <h3>Project Context</h3>
    <p class="lead">
      The Migration Dashboard is an enterprise platform that helps teams configure and manage
      migration projects.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      Originally created for design system migrations, the platform is designed to scale across
      different migration use cases and teams. As adoption grows, enabling users to independently
      create projects becomes essential to reducing reliance on specialized support.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="opportunity" data-comment-id="migration-opportunity">
    <h3>Defining the Opportunity</h3>
    <p class="lead">
      I was tasked with improving the self-service experience for engineers configuring migration
      projects, but without a defined user problem or formal PRD.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      Through user conversations and workflow exploration, I identified a key challenge:
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      Users struggled to understand how configuration decisions connected and how each choice
      influenced the overall migration project.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      Opportunity: How might we provide the right context at the right moment to help users
      confidently navigate complex workflows?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="relationships" data-comment-id="migration-relationships">
    <h3>Making Complex Relationships Clearer</h3>
    <p class="lead">
      Users struggled to understand how Rules, Agents, and Candidate Selection worked together to
      define a migration project.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      I explored different approaches to make these relationships more visible, including combining
      related steps into a single view.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      However, testing revealed that showing everything at once increased cognitive load.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      Final direction: Maintained the progressive step flow while introducing clearer relationships
      between decisions through content, information architecture, and interaction design.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="structure" data-comment-id="migration-structure">
    <h3>Creating Structure in Ambiguity</h3>
    <p class="lead">I established a lightweight design process to move the project forward:</p>
    <ul class="case-study-list">
      <li>Audited the existing experience to identify usability gaps</li>
      <li>Conducted user conversations to understand workflows and pain points</li>
      <li>Defined design priorities based on user needs</li>
      <li>Iterated through concepts with users and cross-functional partners</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="lead">
      The initial experience was rapidly prototyped using AI-assisted workflows. I used AI tools to
      accelerate exploration and prototyping, while relying on research, accessibility principles,
      and design judgment to guide decisions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="takeaway" data-comment-id="migration-takeaway">
    <h3>Takeaway</h3>
    <p class="lead">
      Designing complex workflows is not about removing complexity—it is about helping users
      understand it at the moments where it matters.
    </p>
    <p class="lead">
      This project strengthened my ability to define ambiguous problems, create structure without a
      fixed roadmap, and make intentional design decisions in evolving technical environments.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
